 "sections": [
  {
   "heading": "Saying your name",
   "body": "The usual way to give your name is *Je m'appelle…* (literally \"I call myself…\"): *Je m'appelle Camille.* You can also say *Je suis Camille* (I'm Camille), or *Moi, c'est Camille* (Me, I'm Camille — very common in speech).\n\nTo ask someone's name:\n\n- *Tu t'appelles comment ?* — What's your name? (informal, everyday)\n- *Comment vous appelez-vous ?* — What's your name? (polite)\n- *Il s'appelle comment ?* / *Elle s'appelle comment ?* — What's his / her name?\n\nWhen you meet someone, say *Enchanté* (if you're a man) or *Enchantée* (if you're a woman) — \"delighted\". Both are pronounced the same."
  },
  {
   "heading": "Être — to be",
   "body": "*Être* is irregular, and you'll use it constantly. Learn the whole present tense now:\n\n*Je suis étudiant.* (I'm a student.) *Vous êtes Madame Martin ?* (Are you Mrs Martin?)",
   "table": {
