  {
   "heading": "Subject pronouns",
   "body": "- *je* becomes *j'* before a vowel or silent h: *j'ai*, *j'habite*.\n- *il* = he (or \"it\" for a masculine thing), *elle* = she (or \"it\" for a feminine thing).\n- *ils* = they (men, or a mixed group); *elles* = they (only women or only feminine things).\n- *on* literally means \"one\", but in everyday French it almost always means **we**: *On est à Paris.* (We're in Paris.) It takes the same verb form as *il*.\n- *nous* = we (more formal or written)."
  },
  {
   "heading": "Stressed pronouns: moi, toi…",
   "body": "For emphasis, after *et*, and on their own, French uses *moi* (me), *toi* (you), *lui* (him), *elle* (her), *nous*, *vous*, *eux* (them), *elles*:\n\n- *Moi, c'est Hugo. Et toi ?* — I'm Hugo. And you?\n- *Lui, il s'appelle Thomas.* — He's called Thomas.\n- *Enchanté. — Moi aussi !* — Delighted. — Me too!"
  }
 ],
